<div class="Section1">

  <h1>Root Causes of GST Litigation & Policy Remedies</h1>
  <p style="font-size: 13pt; color: #475569; margin-bottom: 12pt;">
    <strong>An Empirical Investigation Across 5,760+ Advance Ruling Issues (Telangana & All-India)</strong>
  </p>

  <div class="meta-box">
    <table class="meta-table">
      <tr>
        <td class="label">Prepared For:</td>
        <td><strong>Presentation to the Revenue Secretary, Government of India</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="label">Contributors:</td>
        <td>Mahesh Singarapu & Vishal Kumar (Quantana / qlaw)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="label">Data Scope:</td>
        <td>2,845 Advance Rulings (5,763 Issues) All-India | 135 Rulings (258 Issues) Telangana</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="label">Date:</td>
        <td>November 2026</td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
  <p>
    An exhaustive empirical analysis of the nationwide corpus of GST Advance Rulings reveals that <strong>over 80% of litigation is not generated by intentional tax evasion</strong>, but by structural friction embedded in the statutory drafting, fragmented exemption notifications, and discretionary interpretations by field formations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Key highlights from the corpus:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>64.5% of All Disputes</strong> originate from just two categories: <strong>Rate & Exemption (40.0%)</strong> and <strong>Tariff Classification (24.5%)</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Telangana Data Mirrors National Trends:</strong> Telangana exhibits an identical friction pattern, anchored predominantly by <strong>Works Contracts and Infrastructure (48.8% of all TG disputes)</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Dispute Attribution:</strong> Disagreements cluster heavily around ambiguous phrases (e.g. <em>"naturally bundled"</em> in Sec. 8), undefined boundaries (e.g. <em>"Government Entity"</em> in Notification 11/2017), and mischaracterization of commercial trade incentives as non-creditable <em>"gifts"</em> under Section 17(5)(h).</li>
    <li><strong>Systemic Remedy:</strong> Implementing 4 high-leverage policy and administrative interventions can eliminate up to <strong>50%–60% of recurring litigation</strong> before it reaches the High Courts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Empirical Comparison: Telangana (TG) vs. All-India Corpus</h2>
  <p>
    A structured taxonomy tagging all cited provisions and dispute questions reveals the following distribution across the entire national corpus vs. the State of Telangana:
  </p>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Dispute Category</th>
        <th>All-India Count (Share)</th>
        <th>Telangana Count (Share)</th>
